QWasmWindow::setVisible(true) makes the window's DOM node visible but
never requests window activation. As a result a re-shown non-modal
QDialog (e.g. `dialog->show()` for a widget hidden with `dialog->hide()`)
appears with the "inactive" decoration class, doesn't receive keyboard
focus, and can't be interacted with.

Request activation on show for QDialog windows that accept focus. The
`focusWindow() != window()` gate makes it a no-op for already-active
windows, so it doesn't interfere with the initial show cycle. Restricted
to Qt::Dialog so we don't touch tooltips/popups (which have their own
grab-based handling) or WA_ShowWithoutActivating background windows
(which are top-level Qt::Window with focus opted out at the widget layer
and are hard to distinguish at the platform layer).

--- a/src/plugins/platforms/wasm/qwasmwindow.cpp
+++ b/src/plugins/platforms/wasm/qwasmwindow.cpp
@@ -498,6 +498,16 @@ void QWasmWindow::setVisible(bool visible)
 #if QT_CONFIG(accessibility)
         QWasmAccessibility::onShowWindow(window());
 #endif
+        // A re-shown non-modal QDialog otherwise appears "inactive"
+        // (dimmed, no keyboard focus) because show() on an already-created
+        // dialog widget doesn't trigger a fresh activation cycle on wasm.
+        // Force activation when a Dialog transitions to visible and Qt
+        // doesn't already consider it the active window.
+        const bool acceptsFocus = (window()->flags() & Qt::WindowDoesNotAcceptFocus)
+                                  != Qt::WindowDoesNotAcceptFocus;
+        if (window()->type() == Qt::Dialog && acceptsFocus
+            && QGuiApplication::focusWindow() != window())
+            window()->requestActivate();
     }
 }

